# The kit's ignore block: what a Maestro pod writes into /workspace that is not content. The
# launchers refuse a batch on ANY untracked file (state-check.sh, "tree clean & pushed"), so each
# line here is a launch condition, not tidiness. scripts/ignore-block.sh appends the block line by
# line and never removes a line of yours; the bootstrap runs it on every first message.

# Maestro session state: `.maestro/*` and NOT `.maestro/` — git does not descend into an ignored
# directory, so a directory rule would make the negation below unreachable and config.json would
# silently never be committed.
.maestro/*
!.maestro/config.json
# the worker worktrees: spawn-worker.sh puts each at .worktrees/<name>, a real checkout, not content
.worktrees/
# the caches pod-setup.sh points into the workspace volume (uv, Hugging Face, Playwright): gigabytes
.caches/
# the editor's remote server (Cursor / VS Code, serverInstallPath = /workspace)
.cursor-server/
.vscode-server/
# the kit, if it is ever cloned inside the tree by mistake (the bootstrap clones it to ~/.kit)
.kit/
# Maestro's volume seed marker (CLI-created sessions write it into the workspace root)
.pvc-seeded
# this pod's parameters: orchestration/local.env.example documents the keys and IS committed
orchestration/local.env
# environments and test caches, wherever a worker creates them
.venv/
__pycache__/
.pytest_cache/
node_modules/
# secrets live outside the tree (a file under /home/vscode); these catch a stray copy
.env
*.env
*.key
